  data: {
    LLM: "Starting Hand Selection by Position\n\n Position  Playable Hands \n-------------------------\n Early (UTG, UTG+1)  AA, KK, QQ, AKs, AQs \n Middle  Add: JJ, TT, AKo, AJs, KQs, QJs \n Late (Cutoff, Button)  Add: 99-22, ATs-A2s, KTs, suited connectors \n Blinds  Defend with wider range due to pot odds \nKey Principles\n1. Play tight in early position\n2. Loosen up in late position\n3. Raise more than you call\n4. Fold when beat\n5. Don't chase draws without odds\n\nContinuation Betting\nBet flop after raising pre-flop (~60-75% of pot)\nMore effective on dry boards\nLess effective on coordinated/wet boards\nConsider opponent's range\nPot Odds and Equity\nCalculate: Call amount / (Pot + Call amount)\nCompare to chance of winning\nCall when equity > pot odds required\nPosition Play\nAct last = more information\nSteal blinds from late position\nControl pot size in position\nAvoid marginal spots out of position\n\nRange Analysis\nThink in ranges, not specific hands\nConsider what hands opponent would play this way\nBalance your own ranges\nUse blockers to construct bluffs\nBoard Texture\nDry boards (rainbow, disconnected): Bluff more\nWet boards (two-tone, connected): Bet bigger for protection\nDynamic boards: Consider future streets\nStatic boards: Current hand strength matters more\nGame Theory Optimal (GTO) vs Exploitative\nGTO: Unexploitable baseline\nExploitative: Deviate to maximize profit vs mistakes\nAgainst unknowns: Play GTO\nAgainst known mistakes: Exploit",
